"spec.md (lines 190-309 of 402, 19171 bytes)\n   190→(C-Mul)   e₁ ⇓ t₁  and  e₂ ⇓ t₂    ⟹ Mul(e₁, e₂) ⇓ t₁ · STAR  · t₂\n   191→\n   192→(C-Par)   e ⇓ t                    ⟹ e      ⇓ LPAREN · t · RPAREN\n   193→```\n   194→\n   195→**Definition (language).** `t ∈ L(G)`  ⟺  `∃ e. e ⇓ t`.\n   196→\n   197→The EBNF grammar of §4 denotes exactly `L(G)`: every coverage derivation\n   198→replays as a grammar derivation and vice versa (structural induction; the\n   199→paren rule corresponds to the `LPAREN expr RPAREN` production, C-Plus to\n   200→`PLUS factor`).\n   201→\n   202→## 8. Parsing\n   203→\n   204→### 8.1 Operational reference algorithm\n   205→\n   206→`parse : Token* → E ⊎ ParseError` — recursive descent with a cursor over the\n   207→token list; each function returns the built (sub)tree and the unconsumed\n   208→remainder:\n   209→\n   210→```\n   211→parse(t) = let (e, rest) = expr(t) in\n   212→           if rest = ε then e else error UnexpectedToken(head(rest))\n   213→\n   214→expr(t) = let (e, rest) = term(t) in loop where\n   215→  rest = PLUS · rest′  → (e′, rest) ← term(rest′); e ← Add(e, e′); repeat\n   216→  rest = MINUS · rest′ → (e′, rest) ← term(rest′); e ← Sub(e, e′); repeat\n   217→  otherwise            → return (e, rest)\n   218→\n   219→term(t) = let (f, rest) = factor(t) in loop where\n   220→  rest = STAR · rest′  → (f′, rest) ← factor(rest′); f ← Mul(f, f′); repeat\n   221→  otherwise            → return (f, rest)\n   222→\n   223→factor(t) =\n   224→  t = INT(n) · rest  → (Int n, rest)\n   225→  t = LPAREN · rest  → (e, rest′) ← expr(rest)\n   226→                       if rest′ = RPAREN · rest″ then (e, rest″)\n   227→                       else error MissingRPAREN\n   228→  t = PLUS · rest    → factor(rest)                (PLUS consumed, no node)\n   229→  t = MINUS · rest   → (e, rest′) ← factor(rest); (Neg e, rest′)\n   230→  otherwise          → error (UnexpectedEnd if t = ε,\n   231→                              UnexpectedToken(head(t)) otherwise)\n   232→```\n   233→\n   234→**Termination (T0).** `parse` halts on every input: each recursive call and\n   235→each loop iteration strictly advances the cursor (a token is consumed before\n   236→every descent), so the unconsumed remainder shrinks; `parse` is therefore a\n   237→total function `Token* → E ⊎ ParseError`.\n   238→\n   239→**Soundness (T1).** If `parse(t) = e` then `e ⇓ t`.\n   240→*(Induction on `|t|`; each construction step mirrors one coverage rule.)*\n   241→\n   242→**Completeness (T2).** If some `e ⇓ t` then `parse(t) = e`.\n   243→*(Induction on `|t|` using the LL(1) disjointness of `FIRST(factor)` and the\n   244→forced continuations of `expr`/`term`; the algorithm's choices are the only\n   245→ones compatible with a covering derivation.)*\n   246→\n   247→**Uniqueness (T3, corollary of T1 + T2).** If `e ⇓ t` and `e′ ⇓ t` then\n   248→`e = e′`; the grammar is unambiguous and `parse` computes *the* covering AST.\n   249→\n   250→Consequently `L(G) = { t : parse(t) succeeds }`, and `parse` fails exactly on\n   251→`t ∉ L(G)`. Parse failures are precisely: end of input where an operand was\n   252→required; a token that cannot begin a `factor` (`STAR`, `RPAREN`); a missing\n   253→`RPAREN` for an opened group; leftover tokens after the top-level `expr`.\n   254→\n   255→**[N]** Any conforming parser must be observationally equivalent to the\n   256→algorithm above (same accept/reject decision and same AST on acceptance) or,\n   257→more weakly, satisfy §9–§10 directly.\n   258→\n   259→## 9. Top-level semantics of `evaluate`\n   260→\n   261→For `s ∈ String`, with `lex` (§3.3), `parse` (§8.1), `eval` (§6):\n   262→\n   263→```\n   264→evaluate(s) = eval(parse(t))    if lex(s) = t  and  t ∈ L(G)\n   265→evaluate(s) = ↯ ValueError      if lex(s) fails (LexError)\n   266→evaluate(s) = ↯ ValueError      if lex(s) = t and t ∉ L(G)\n   267→```\n   268→\n   269→The value case is well-defined because the covering AST is unique (T3).\n   270→\n   271→Normative contract:\n   272→\n   273→- **C1 (Termination).** `evaluate(s)` halts for every `s ∈ String`.\n   274→- **C2 (Result type).** `evaluate(s)` returns a Python `int` (equivalently, a\n   275→  value of ℤ) iff `lex(s)` succeeds and `lex(s) ∈ L(G)`; the value equals\n   276→  `eval(e)` for the unique `e` covering `lex(s)`.\n   277→- **C3 (Error exactness).** `evaluate(s)` raises `ValueError` iff `lex(s)`\n   278→  fails or `lex(s) ∉ L(G)`. No other exception may escape for `str` inputs\n   279→  within the resource envelope of §12.\n   280→- **C4 (Messages).** Exception *messages* are not contractual. Indicative\n   281→  strings of the reference implementation:\n   282→\n   283→  | Condition                                   | Indicative message                     |\n   284→  |---------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n   285→  | invalid character `c` at 0-based index `i`  | `Invalid character {c!r} at position {i}` |\n   286→  | operand expected at end of input            | `Unexpected end of expression`         |\n   287→  | token where an operand was expected         | `Unexpected token {tok!r}`             |\n   288→  | group not closed                            | `Missing closing parenthesis`          |\n   289→  | leftover tokens after the top-level `expr`  | `Unexpected token {tok!r}`             |\n   290→\n   291→  **[N]** The reference implementation lexes the whole string before parsing,\n   292→  so a lex error anywhere in `s` is reported even where a parse error would\n   293→  occur earlier positionally. Since both surface as `ValueError`, this\n   294→  precedence is unobservable at the level of C2/C3.\n   295→\n   296→## 10. Verification obligations\n   297→\n   298→An implementation conforms to this specification iff it satisfies C1–C4 and\n   299→properties P1–P9 below. Each property is stated so it can be discharged\n   300→directly (e.g. by proof or mechanized testing against the oracle of §11).\n   301→\n   302→- **P1 (Totality & outcome type).** For every `s ∈ String`, evaluating\n   303→  `evaluate(s)` halts and either yields an integer or raises `ValueError`;\n   304→  no other exception escapes.\n   305→- **P2 (Purity & determinism).** The outcome (value or exception) is a\n   306→  function of `s` alone; evaluation has no side effects.\n   307→- **P3 (Semantic agreement — main theorem).** If `lex(s) = t` and\n   308→  `parse(t) = e`, then `evaluate(s) = eval(e)`. Conversely, if\n   309→  `evaluate(s) = n`, then `t = lex(s)` exists and `n = eval(e)` for the"